    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-314735",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9911dc0d-905d-54e1-aadb-f4fbce6f89d2",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating an Inverse Tangent Function",
      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x-4)+2.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x-4)+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(5x-4)}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-4)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(5x-4)}^{2}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+{(5x-4)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
