    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-19080",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9911dc0d-905d-54e1-aadb-f4fbce6f89d2",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating an Inverse Tangent Function",
      "problem": "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={arctan}^{2}⁡(3{e}^{x}).$",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({arctan}^{2}⁡(3{e}^{x})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+{(3{e}^{x})}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+9{e}^{2x}}\\cdot 3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})}{1+9{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
