    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-343769",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9911dc0d-905d-54e1-aadb-f4fbce6f89d2",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating an Inverse Tangent Function",
      "problem": "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}-2x)$ at $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}-2x)] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+({x}^{2}-2x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1+{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}}\\cdot (2x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{6x-6}{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(2)-6}{(2)^{4}-4(2)^{3}+4(2)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "6"
            },
